15 0 obj
<< /A 42 0 R /Alt (Glycosylation analysis showing three bar graphs for peptide sequences, each labeled with their sequence and glycosylation sites at N864, N893, and N1011. Glycoforms are visually represented above each bar, with varying heights indicating different percentage fractions. A legend clarifies symbols for Neu5Ac, Galactose, GlcNAc, Mannose, and Fucose, along with a blue triangle marking the occupied N-glycosylation site. Below, numbered circles show the sequence positions with blue triangles pointing to sites with glycosylation data.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 32 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
16 0 obj
<< /A 43 0 R /Alt (A composite image with multiple graphs: \(A\) A Venn diagram showing overlap between SP-HUS and healthy controls, with 15 unique to SP-HUS and 7 shared. \(B\) A bar graph depicting glycoform fractions in the acute phase for different groups, indicating significant changes. \(C\) A similar bar graph for the remission phase, showing variations in glycoform fractions. \(D\) A scores plot from a principal component analysis, differentiating SP-HUS and control groups. \(E\) A loadings plot highlighting specific glycoforms, represented by labels like H4N4S1 and H5N4. Each graph uses color coding to represent different participant groups.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 33 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
17 0 obj
<< /A 44 0 R /Alt (Grid chart comparing N-glycan profiles across healthy controls and four participant groups \(P1 to P4\) with variations labeled as A and R. Categories include complete complex N-glycans and losses in sialic acid, galactose, and antennae. A color scale from red to blue indicates percentage variations, with red for increases and blue for decreases. N864, N893, and N1011 are shown with specific N-glycan expressions.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 34 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
18 0 obj
<< /A 45 0 R /Alt (Panel A displays a line graph depicting the percentage of lysis against micrograms per milliliter of Factor H, showing four lines: wild type Factor H, negative control, dFH, and FH. The negative control maintains a high lysis percentage, while the others decrease significantly. Panel B shows a bar graph of percentage iC3b formation for the same categories. The wild type FH and negative control have significantly different results, while dFH and FH show no significant difference. Error bars indicate variability, and significance levels are annotated with asterisks.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 35 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
19 0 obj
<< /A 46 0 R /Alt (Panel A features line graphs showing percentage lysis in relation to microgram per milliliter of Factor H for four patients during active and remission phases, with comparisons to wild type FH and a negative control. Panel B presents a bar graph illustrating the percentage of iC3b formation in patients during acute and remission stages, compared to wild type FH and a negative control. Variations are marked with asterisks indicating statistical significance.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 36 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
20 0 obj
<< /A 47 0 R /Alt (Graphs illustrating the effect of Factor H on % lysis and neuraminidase activity.   A: Line graph showing % lysis decreases with increasing concentrations of Factor H for two conditions, dFH and FH. dFH shows a higher % lysis compared to FH.  B: Bar graph depicting neuraminidase activity increasing with higher Factor H concentrations in the dFH condition, while FH remains low.  C: Line graph comparing % lysis for dFH and dFH with neuraminidase. Neuraminidase increases lysis percentage at similar Factor H concentrations. Error bars represent variability.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 37 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
